202
00:18:51,760 --> 00:18:58,160
 whether machine learning models can take both audio content, what people say in the verbal modalities,

203
00:18:58,160 --> 00:19:03,760
 and also visual content. So people's gestures and expressions in the nonverbal modalities.

204
00:19:03,760 --> 00:19:09,440
 And really trying to understand whether you can infer emotions from these modalities.

205
00:19:09,440 --> 00:19:16,160
 Some of the annotations include both discrete emotions, spanning happiness,

206
00:19:16,160 --> 00:19:21,760
 sadness, and surprise, and also continuous emotions, which might be a term new to some of you.

207
00:19:21,760 --> 00:19:28,800
 Continuous emotions are real value dimensions of emotion that range across continuous numbers,

208
00:19:28,800 --> 00:19:33,440
 that range across a spectrum. So one spectrum which people analyze is a spectrum from positive to

209
00:19:33,440 --> 00:19:39,840
 negative that is called valence. Another spectrum which people analyze is that of active to passive

210
00:19:39,840 --> 00:19:44,320
 that is called arousal. So for a lot of these data sets, they are looking at both valence,

211
00:19:44,320 --> 00:19:49,840
 positive to negative, and arousal, active to passive. And really getting human annotators to

212
00:19:49,840 --> 00:19:55,280
 annotate these on a continuous spectrum. So it's a regression problem. And the goal is to

213
00:19:55,280 --> 00:20:00,480
 take in this audio visual content and be able to predict both your discrete and continuous emotions.

214
00:20:00,480 --> 00:20:07,520
 Some of the recent data sets also have transcripts. So you can process text without actually looking

215
00:20:07,520 --> 00:20:15,680
 at the audio features. And there's also been some extensions of this. Some of them in other

216
00:20:15,680 --> 00:20:22,000
 languages like German. Some of them more focused on human-computer interaction. And some of these

217
00:20:22,000 --> 00:20:30,080
 are more popular than others. One really interesting data set is this one called the Recola data set.

218
00:20:30,080 --> 00:20:34,400
 It's really popular, firstly, for a couple of reasons. First, it contains very fine-grained

219
00:20:34,400 --> 00:20:40,160
 annotations at the frame level. So some of the data sets I'm going to be describing to you

220
00:20:40,160 --> 00:20:45,600
 have an entire video. And after you finish the entire video of someone speaking, you have a

221
00:20:45,600 --> 00:20:51,840
 single annotation for that entire video. For the Recola data set, you have this entire video,

222
00:20:51,840 --> 00:20:57,360
 and you actually have valence at every frame. So you have these temporal labels. In this case,

223
00:20:57,360 --> 00:21:02,160
 these are continuous emotions of arousal and valence. And essentially, you have a slider

224
00:21:02,160 --> 00:21:06,000
 of these continuous emotions that goes up and down across the frames in the video.

225
00:21:06,640 --> 00:21:11,360
 So these are really, really fine-grained labels that add in further challenges, which I've made

226
00:21:11,360 --> 00:21:16,400
 this data set. A very interesting one for people to study. Another interesting aspect is that

227
00:21:16,400 --> 00:21:23,040
 apart from both audio and visual features, it also includes physiological data. So one of them is

228
00:21:23,040 --> 00:21:29,280
 electrocardiography, which is a graph of voltage versus time of the electrical activity of the heart.

229
00:21:29,280 --> 00:21:33,520
 So how fast the heart is pumping? And that is using some electrodes on the skin.

230
00:21:35,120 --> 00:21:40,960
 EDA is another physiological signal that measures how much a person is sweating.

231
00:21:40,960 --> 00:21:46,800
 Suppose no sweating and heart rates have been seen in psychology research to be predictors

232
00:21:46,800 --> 00:21:51,360
 of emotion. And the goal here is also to see where the machine learning models are able to

233
00:21:51,360 --> 00:21:57,200
 accurately integrate both your verbal, nonverbal features, and also these physiological data

234
00:21:57,200 --> 00:22:06,080
 sources to be able to get better predictions. So a lot of these data sets in effective computing

235
00:22:06,080 --> 00:22:11,280
 have been around for longer, just because effective computing is an earlier field that started in

236
00:22:11,280 --> 00:22:15,600
 both psychology and statistical analysis before moving into this deep learning era.

237
00:22:15,600 --> 00:22:20,560
 And it's always a trade-off. So a lot of these data sets in effective computing have been along

238
00:22:20,560 --> 00:22:26,560
 for some time. The benefit is that there's a lot of work in both data-driven and also psychology

239
00:22:26,560 --> 00:22:33,680
 research in studying these problems. But the main drawback is that you then have to work harder to

240
00:22:33,680 --> 00:22:45,680
 find out ways to improve these problems. These are data sets. Cool. Some of the -- also talk about

241
00:22:45,680 --> 00:22:51,600
 some of the research that has come out of our group. So our group has really focused on multimodal

242
00:22:51,600 --> 00:22:57,200
 sentiment analysis. So sentiment is a concept of language, and people have studied this in linguistics.

243
00:22:57,200 --> 00:23:02,640
 Essentially, whether a person is reflecting positively or negatively to a particular video.

244
00:23:02,640 --> 00:23:08,480
 This started off as a main research project in NLP. Language is clearly very important if I

245
00:23:08,480 --> 00:23:12,800
 express something like, you know, I went to the restaurant and I love the food. The service was

246
00:23:12,800 --> 00:23:19,120
 awesome. It's clear that I'm giving a very subjectively positive opinion about this -- about a topic

247
00:23:19,120 --> 00:23:24,320
 at hand, which is this restaurant. Language kind of offers most of the heavy lifting, offers most of

248
00:23:24,320 --> 00:23:30,320
 the information in sentiment analysis. But we also realized that people also tend to express their

249
00:23:30,320 --> 00:23:36,960
 opinions in novel gestures. So things like, you know, sartasm. Things like just being ambivalent

250
00:23:36,960 --> 00:23:43,760
 in language by, you know, showing visibly discontent, visible discontent using your verbal -- non-verbal

251
00:23:43,760 --> 00:23:50,240
 gestures. So what we've done is we extended some of this work in language-based sentiment analysis

252
00:23:50,240 --> 00:23:55,280
 to these multimodal sources. We've collected some videos on YouTube spanning many speakers and

253
00:23:55,280 --> 00:24:02,400
 spanning a bunch of opinion videos. And the goal is to take in these language visual and acoustic

254
00:24:02,400 --> 00:24:09,440
 data and make a prediction about human sentiment. We've also since then expanded this data set.

255
00:24:09,440 --> 00:24:14,080
 So now this is one of the largest data sets in multimodal learning that spans almost 25,000

256
00:24:14,080 --> 00:24:20,160
 video segments, thousands of speakers and 250 topics. And we've also extended the annotations

257
00:24:20,160 --> 00:24:26,000
 from both fine-grained sentiment, five-class sentiment to a lot of these emotions, these discrete emotions

258
00:24:26,000 --> 00:24:34,160
 as well. At the same time, people have also extended the research on both -- from single person to

259
00:24:34,160 --> 00:24:39,200
 multiparty emotional recognition. So this male data set is a data set that's collected from the

260
00:24:39,200 --> 00:24:45,280
 French TV show. And you're annotating the emotions of each of the characters separately. So the

261
00:24:45,280 --> 00:24:50,320
 goal here is not just to look at the overall sentiment and emotions of the entire conversation,

262
00:24:50,320 --> 00:24:56,880
 but rather to keep these baseline models of how each individual is progressing in terms of their

263
00:24:56,880 --> 00:25:03,920
 emotions. In this case, one of the characters is always showing positive emotion, joy and surprise.

264
00:25:04,400 --> 00:25:16,960
 While the other person is showing more anger. Cool. So I have a question here. I always think

265
00:25:16,960 --> 00:25:22,560
 that defining emotional state will require combinations of some time period of facial landmarks, gestures

266
00:25:22,560 --> 00:25:28,080
 and eyes across multiple frames. So does it make sense to have emotional labels at a frame level?

267
00:25:28,080 --> 00:25:33,760
 That's a great question. So this really ties into what I'm going to talk a bit next,

268
00:25:33,760 --> 00:25:38,800
 which is what are the core challenges most involved in affect recognition. So I think the main

269
00:25:38,800 --> 00:25:43,280
 question here is that -- is that of the alignment problem? Because you have these really long-term

270
00:25:43,280 --> 00:25:47,680
 videos. You have language, you have visual and acoustic, and a lot of these features are not

271
00:25:47,680 --> 00:25:53,760
 necessarily going to be aligned. If I'm saying something positive at time five, I'm most likely

272
00:25:53,760 --> 00:25:59,520
 going to smile at some other time. Maybe I was smiling or two seconds later. And maybe I will

273
00:25:59,520 --> 00:26:04,400
 even laugh maybe a few seconds later. So there's this alignment problem where not all the modalities

274
00:26:04,400 --> 00:26:09,360
 are perfectly aligned at the same time level. So whether it makes sense to have annotations

275
00:26:09,360 --> 00:26:15,040
 at that particular frame level. So I think a way that people deal with this or there's two aspects.

276
00:26:15,040 --> 00:26:18,720
 First of all, you have to deal with your alignment problem, which is a big problem in research,

277
00:26:18,720 --> 00:26:23,280
 regardless of whether you want to have predictions at a frame level or you want to have predictions

278
00:26:23,280 --> 00:26:28,960
 at a video level. Alignment is one of those problems, which is always there. In terms of

279
00:26:28,960 --> 00:26:35,520
 actually having emotions at a frame level, what people do tend to do is that you want

280
00:26:35,520 --> 00:26:39,280
 to annotate -- when these annotators are looking at these videos, they're kind of having -- they

281
00:26:39,280 --> 00:26:45,120
 have a sliding bar. So the frame, the annotation for the frame t equals to five is actually looking

282
00:26:45,120 --> 00:26:50,560
 at the information before t equals to five. So you want to keep a rolling average of the

283
00:26:50,560 --> 00:26:57,680
 features that you have seen so far and use that to update your frame level emotion annotation.

284
00:26:58,640 --> 00:27:02,960
 So in this case, it's kind of simplifying the alignment problem to only having to reason about

285
00:27:02,960 --> 00:27:09,600
 information that has happened in the previous time steps in the video. But these are awesome

286
00:27:09,600 --> 00:27:13,920
 questions. These are kind of the research questions that you should be looking at. Basically,

287
00:27:13,920 --> 00:27:18,480
 not just looking at the data set itself, but really challenging some of the hypotheses and

288
00:27:18,480 --> 00:27:23,280
 assumptions and research questions that came out when this -- when the authors of these data sets

289
00:27:23,280 --> 00:27:31,600
 built them. So going on that similar vein, when everyone's working on these tasks,

290
00:27:31,600 --> 00:27:37,520
 I want everyone to think about what are the main challenges in modeling these problems,

291
00:27:37,520 --> 00:27:41,840
 not just trying to get better results on the data set, but really thinking about some of the

292
00:27:41,840 --> 00:27:46,880
 research questions. So for affect recognition, a lot of the main challenges comes in fusing

293
00:27:46,880 --> 00:27:51,520
 these modalities. You have multiple data sources, some of which are more useful than others at

294
00:27:51,520 --> 00:27:55,600
 different time periods. The goal is really to how to best leverage all of these data sources to

295
00:27:55,600 --> 00:28:01,760
 make a prediction. At the same time, you need to have suitable levels of extraction to get really

296
00:28:01,760 --> 00:28:06,960
 good multimodal representations. And you also need alignment. So in this case, because most of these

297
00:28:06,960 --> 00:28:12,320
 data sets are temporal data sets consisting of videos, alignment is a very big challenge where

298
00:28:12,320 --> 00:28:20,000
 you have to align positive words in text with smiles and gestures and the visual modalities.

299
00:28:22,480 --> 00:28:27,600
 Cold learning is something that people don't usually study, but it's also an area which has

300
00:28:27,600 --> 00:28:32,880
 arisen in affective computing. And there was one example which I'll be talked about last week,

301
00:28:32,880 --> 00:28:38,320
 which is to look at language-based sentiment analysis. But during training, you kind of enriching

302
00:28:38,320 --> 00:28:44,240
 language with visual and audio behaviors. If you recall that with the model where you took language

303
00:28:44,240 --> 00:28:51,360
 and you're trying to construct these audio and visual behaviors to make language more informative.

304
00:28:51,680 --> 00:28:58,000
 But at test time, you only use a language. So that is one example of cold learning. And

305
00:28:58,000 --> 00:29:01,280
 I have seen people apply cold learning to problems in affect recognition.

306
00:29:01,280 --> 00:29:16,640
 Cool. At this point, I'm going to go into two examples of past research projects from

307
00:29:16,640 --> 00:29:23,120
 taken by students taking this course on affective computing. So one of these research projects

308
00:29:23,120 --> 00:29:28,640
 is called select additive learning. And the goal here is to perform better on multimodal

309
00:29:28,640 --> 00:29:33,920
 sentiment analysis across these data sets. And the main idea is to reduce the effect of confounding

310
00:29:33,920 --> 00:29:39,440
 factors. So what are these confounding factors? Let me give you an example. So suppose you have

311
00:29:39,440 --> 00:29:44,960
 a data set like this. You have each of these are video clips and they're saying something and,

312
00:29:44,960 --> 00:29:51,680
 you know, each of these speakers are saying multiple utterances. Some of them are positive,

313
00:29:51,680 --> 00:29:57,440
 some of them are negative, annotated in the MOSI data set. So the main problem in machine learning

314
00:29:57,440 --> 00:30:02,800
 research is to find what rules you can infer from this data. Rules being a mapping from

315
00:30:02,800 --> 00:30:08,560
 input features to labels that you want to predict. So if you look at this, for example,

316
00:30:08,560 --> 00:30:12,880
 you see the first person is smiling, the third person is smiling, and they consist an

317
00:30:12,880 --> 00:30:18,880
 usual positive sentiment. So one rule that you could infer from training these supervised learning

318
00:30:18,880 --> 00:30:24,400
 models is that the presence of a smile leads to positive sentiment. Another rule that you

319
00:30:24,400 --> 00:30:29,280
 couldn't infer based on, you know, this person and this person down here is that the presence of a

320
00:30:29,280 --> 00:30:33,360
 frown indicates negative sentiment. And these are very reasonable rules that most likely would

321
00:30:33,360 --> 00:30:40,080
 generalize. Likewise, you can see that a nod might refer to positive sentiment. But if you look at

322
00:30:40,080 --> 00:30:45,360
 this data set, another rule that you couldn't infer is that because this person is wearing glasses

323
00:30:45,360 --> 00:30:50,480
 and this person is wearing glasses. And so is this person and they all occur with the fact that

324
00:30:50,480 --> 00:30:55,200
 they're saying something that contains negative sentiment. A possible rule that you couldn't infer

325
00:30:55,200 --> 00:31:00,720
 that would still be consistent with this data set is that the presence of glasses worn by a person

326
00:31:00,720 --> 00:31:05,920
 leads to negative sentiment. If you use such a rule and you train them on this data set,

327
00:31:05,920 --> 00:31:11,040
 you will still get really good training and test error. But this is what we call a confounding

328
00:31:11,040 --> 00:31:16,160
 factor that most likely will not generalize. So essentially, this is an artifact in the data

329
00:31:16,160 --> 00:31:24,800
 collection process. So what the students proposed was to build a solution that learns representations

330
00:31:24,800 --> 00:31:30,080
 that kind of masks out the effect of user identity. Because a lot of they went through these data sets

331
00:31:30,080 --> 00:31:34,160
 and found that a lot of these confounding factors actually had to do with these, you know,

332
00:31:34,160 --> 00:31:38,320
 person-specific things, you know, whether a person is wearing glasses, whether a person, you know,

333
00:31:38,320 --> 00:31:44,160
 is male or female, has, you know, black or brown hair. So what they did was to modify the conventional

334
00:31:44,160 --> 00:31:48,400
 representation learning approach that goes on features through a model to your labels,

335
00:31:48,400 --> 00:31:55,280
 to a new model that takes the features, learns the representations, but at the same time also

336
00:31:55,280 --> 00:32:01,200
 takes in identities, learns the representation, and adding noise to the representation of these

337
00:32:01,200 --> 00:32:08,640
 identities. The hypothesis here is that the representation of the representation that is

338
00:32:08,640 --> 00:32:14,000
 most useful in predicting a label should come from these person-independent features, you know,

339
00:32:14,000 --> 00:32:18,880
 things that whether you're actually smiling or nodding, and these person-dependent factors,

340
00:32:18,880 --> 00:32:23,600
 which actually should not take part in making a prediction, things like whether you're actually

341
00:32:23,600 --> 00:32:28,800
 wearing glasses or what color your hair is. So by adding noise and sampling various amounts of

342
00:32:28,800 --> 00:32:34,160
 noise across this data set, you're actually marginalizing the effect of speakers out from the model.

343
00:32:34,160 --> 00:32:39,840
 You're making sure that a model is not really taking into account the identity of the speaker

344
00:32:39,840 --> 00:32:44,800
 and still being able to focus on the real useful input features towards making this prediction.

345
00:32:44,800 --> 00:32:53,280
 Right, so this was a course project done I think about three years ago, and this was published in

346
00:32:53,280 --> 00:33:00,960
 this multimedia conference. Another research project, which is also of course a multimodal sentiment

347
00:33:00,960 --> 00:33:07,200
 analysis, is that of solving the alignment and both fusion and alignment problem. So the goal

348
00:33:07,200 --> 00:33:14,000
 is to estimate the importance of each modality at the word level in a video. So in terms of fusion,

349
00:33:14,000 --> 00:33:20,400
 the goal is to kind of take this video and break it apart into multiple frames, and for each frame

350
00:33:20,400 --> 00:33:26,000
 we're going to reason about whether the language, visual, and acoustic gestures are actually useful.

351
00:33:26,000 --> 00:33:31,040
 So in this case, you see that this speaker is only showing a smile at this time step,

352
00:33:31,040 --> 00:33:36,400
 and what we should do is build a gate that lets these visual features pass into the model

353
00:33:36,400 --> 00:33:41,920
 to predict that this person is showing positive sentiment. And the other times of the video where

354
00:33:41,920 --> 00:33:46,880
 the person is not showing any visible gestures from visual modality, we should not use this visual

355
00:33:46,880 --> 00:33:54,800
 modality towards making a prediction. So the goal is to build a model that is more interpretable,

356
00:33:54,800 --> 00:34:00,800
 that estimates modality and temporal importance, and then is to attend to this important information,

357
00:34:00,800 --> 00:34:08,480
 both across modalities and across time. And our solution here was a couple of components in this

358
00:34:08,480 --> 00:34:14,480
 model. One of the main components is to be the first one to do a word level alignment. So we're

359
00:34:14,480 --> 00:34:20,960
 actually segmenting, we're defining a reasonable approximation to segment the video into the level

360
00:34:20,960 --> 00:34:26,080
 of words. So we're going to take all of these words, each of them correspond to one time step,

361
00:34:26,080 --> 00:34:30,400
 and we're going to take the audio and visual gestures and align them with respect to these words.

362
00:34:30,400 --> 00:34:35,840
 So the basic assumption here is that whatever is you're going to say a word,

363
00:34:35,840 --> 00:34:39,680
 and we're also going to attend to the visual and audio gestures that were set

364
00:34:39,680 --> 00:34:47,120
 during this same duration. And in terms of estimating the importance, we have both a temporal attention,

365
00:34:47,120 --> 00:34:51,680
 which estimates the importance of each time step, certain time steps might correspond more

366
00:34:51,680 --> 00:34:57,600
 towards predicting a label, if that time step has a very visibly important word or gesture,

367
00:34:57,600 --> 00:35:03,200
 and other time steps will correspond less. And for these other modalities like audio and visual,

368
00:35:03,200 --> 00:35:09,120
 we also have this gated attention that is a binary game that is either 0 or 1, which either

369
00:35:09,120 --> 00:35:13,440
 lets these are audio visual features go through, if the model estimates that they are important

370
00:35:13,440 --> 00:35:24,720
 words predicting a label, and 0 otherwise. So the hypothesis here is that the attention

371
00:35:24,720 --> 00:35:28,880
 weights represent the contribution of each modality at each time step, and likewise,

372
00:35:28,880 --> 00:35:33,040
 the modality gates is meant to determine the importance and contribution of each modality.

373
00:35:33,040 --> 00:35:38,400
 And because these gates are 0 and 1, they're not continuous numbers that you can back propagate

374
00:35:38,400 --> 00:35:42,000
 through. We're actually training these gates using reinforcement learning, and that's one of the

375
00:35:42,000 --> 00:35:52,800
 main technical contributions in this paper. Cool. So those are two examples of cross projects that

376
00:35:52,800 --> 00:35:59,840
 were done in previous years on effective computing. Now I'm going to jump into media description.

377
00:36:04,240 --> 00:36:09,680
 So as you recall, in this computational and deep learning era, media description, given an image

378
00:36:09,680 --> 00:36:13,680
 like a video, like videos and audio saying being able to accurately caption the image,

379
00:36:13,680 --> 00:36:19,520
 was one of the main enablers of modern multimodal machine learning research, both in terms of

380
00:36:19,520 --> 00:36:24,880
 really large data sets that have images and captions, and a lot of these data sets are also obtained

381
00:36:24,880 --> 00:36:29,760
 from the internet. For example, a lot of these data sets are from things like Instagram, Flickr,

382
00:36:30,320 --> 00:36:34,400
 where you have where people just upload these images and provide some user-generated captions

383
00:36:34,400 --> 00:36:42,080
 for these. So these large data sets progress in modeling really enabled this modern boom in

384
00:36:42,080 --> 00:36:47,280
 multimodal machine learning research. And one of the biggest data sets that enabled this was the

385
00:36:47,280 --> 00:36:55,440
 MS-COCO data set. This one was really large, 120,000 images, and each of these images was taken

386
00:36:55,440 --> 00:37:01,920
 onto Amazon Mechanical Turk and annotated with five different captions. And it's really important

387
00:37:01,920 --> 00:37:09,840
 to get high quality annotations that span many captions, because this problem requires you to

388
00:37:09,840 --> 00:37:14,400
 reason about many different captions that could be generated from the same image.

389
00:37:14,400 --> 00:37:23,520
 The main challenge here, again, is that of evaluation. Many of these generation tasks

390
00:37:23,520 --> 00:37:28,560
 is very difficult to evaluate what makes a good caption and what makes a bad caption. You don't

391
00:37:28,560 --> 00:37:33,360
 want to necessarily just constrain yourself with something like supervised learning, where you just

392
00:37:33,360 --> 00:37:38,400
 try to match the captions exactly, because there's just so many ways a human could caption an image,

393
00:37:38,400 --> 00:37:43,440
 and many of these could still be reasonable captions despite not being in the data set.

394
00:37:48,640 --> 00:37:55,440
 So when these people released these data set, they also had an evaluation server. And the goal

395
00:37:55,440 --> 00:38:00,160
 here was to at least try their best to take this candidate sentence and evaluate against a set

396
00:38:00,160 --> 00:38:07,600
 of ground truth sentences using either human or evaluation metrics. And what they did at CDPR 2015

397
00:38:07,600 --> 00:38:13,520
 was that they actually tested a lot of these models if people submitted, and they did a very

398
00:38:13,520 --> 00:38:19,520
 exhaustive test using human annotations. They actually gave these images and captions to humans

399
00:38:19,520 --> 00:38:23,920
 on enter, and they had humans look at whether these captions actually made sense.

400
00:38:23,920 --> 00:38:33,840
 And not surprisingly, the answer was no. A lot of these models done by Google, Microsoft,

401
00:38:33,840 --> 00:38:38,320
 a lot of these big companies, they had very good models, but a lot of it was still quite far from

